# ===========================================================================
# HOW TO READ THIS FILE
#
# Variables are grouped as PRODUCTION CONFIG (set them for a real deployment)
# or DEV-ONLY (they exist for local development and simulation; setting them in
# production is either meaningless or actively wrong — each says which).
#
# Integration CREDENTIALS are not in this file at all. Since Task 3.4 a
# tenant's provider secrets live encrypted in that tenant's own database schema
# and are entered through the API. See the M-Pesa section below.
# ===========================================================================

# --- PRODUCTION CONFIG -----------------------------------------------------

# Prisma connection string. Point this at the platform-level Postgres instance;
# tenant schemas are provisioned within it (schema-per-tenant isolation).
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/angaza?schema=public"

# Fastify server
# NODE_ENV: "development" (default) | "test" | "production". It gates the API
# docs (see API_DOCS_ENABLED below) — set it to production in production.
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=4000
HOST=0.0.0.0
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# OpenAPI docs at /docs and the raw document at /docs/json (Task G.3).
# Default: ON when NODE_ENV is not production, OFF when it is. An interactive
# explorer for a financial API is not a public asset — it enumerates every route
# and field and hands an attacker a working request builder.
#   unset + production      -> /docs does not exist (404)
#   API_DOCS_ENABLED=true   -> /docs exists; in production it ALSO requires an
#                              authenticated session (401 otherwise)
#   API_DOCS_ENABLED=false  -> /docs never exists, in any environment
# Serving docs in production is therefore always a deliberate, auditable act.
API_DOCS_ENABLED=true

# Auth / session layer (Task 0.3)
# JWT_SECRET must be at least 32 characters. Generate a strong value with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(64).toString('hex'))"
# NEVER commit the real secret — this placeholder is illustrative only.
JWT_SECRET=replace-with-a-64-byte-hex-secret-at-least-32-chars-long
ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=900
REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=604800
LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=5
LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
# SENSITIVE rate-limit tier (Task T-A2): secret writes and report generation, per IP per route.
SENSITIVE_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=30
SENSITIVE_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000

# --- Notifications (Task T-A3) -------------------------------------------------
# Which implementation each channel uses (a DEPLOYMENT property). Default: simulated
# (no network). Live providers read their credentials from the encrypted platform
# store (platform_integration_credentials), NEVER from env.
NOTIFICATION_SMS_PROVIDER=simulated
NOTIFICATION_EMAIL_PROVIDER=simulated

# --- Password recovery (Task T-A4) ---------------------------------------------
# How long a password reset code stays usable, in minutes.
PASSWORD_RESET_TTL_MINUTES=60

# --- Staff invitations (Task T-A5) ---------------------------------------------
# How long an invitation stays usable, in hours.
INVITATION_TTL_HOURS=72

# --- DEV-ONLY --------------------------------------------------------------
# Seed script (src/scripts/seed-users.ts). DEV-ONLY: this password is public and
# is for local development. Never run the seed with it anywhere a real user
# could authenticate.
SEED_PASSWORD=ChangeMe-Dev-2026

# --- PRODUCTION CONFIG (continued) -----------------------------------------

# Integration credential store (Task 3.4). A 32-byte AES-256-GCM key, supplied
# base64 or hex, that encrypts EVERY per-tenant provider credential at rest.
# Required — the process refuses to start without it, and refuses a key that
# does not decode to exactly 32 bytes. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# Rotating this value makes every already-stored blob undecryptable: rotation
# means re-encrypting the blobs under the new key (and updating their keyRef),
# never editing this line in isolation. NEVER commit the real key.
CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=replace-with-32-random-bytes-base64

# M-Pesa (Task 3.3; made per-tenant by Task 3.4).
#
# CREDENTIALS ARE NO LONGER ENV. A tenant's consumer key, consumer secret,
# passkey and shortcode live encrypted in THAT tenant's own schema
# (tenant_integration_credentials) and are entered through
# PUT /v1/integrations/MPESA/credentials. A process-wide credential set cannot
# be correct in a multi-tenant system: each shop's sales must settle to that
# shop's own shortcode.
#
# What remains here are properties of the DEPLOYMENT, not of a tenant:
#   MPESA_PROVIDER: "simulated" (default, dev) or "daraja" (real Safaricom).
#   MPESA_CALLBACK_PATH_SECRET: required in BOTH modes — the unguessable path
#     segment on the public callback URL. Regenerate with:
#       node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(24).toString('hex'))"
#
# PRODUCTION CONFIG here: MPESA_PROVIDER, MPESA_CALLBACK_PATH_SECRET,
# MPESA_CALLBACK_BASE_URL.
# DEV-ONLY here: MPESA_SIM_DELAY_MS and MPESA_SHORTCODE (both marked below).
MPESA_PROVIDER=simulated
MPESA_CALLBACK_PATH_SECRET=replace-with-a-24-byte-hex-secret-min-16-chars
# DEV-ONLY: pacing for the simulated provider. Ignored under MPESA_PROVIDER=daraja.
MPESA_SIM_DELAY_MS=1500
# REQUIRED only when MPESA_PROVIDER=daraja: the public origin Safaricom can
# reach. The full per-tenant callback URL is server-generated from it as
#   <origin>/v1/webhooks/mpesa/<MPESA_CALLBACK_PATH_SECRET>/<tenantSlug>
# The Daraja API host is NOT configured — it is derived from each tenant's
# active environment (SANDBOX -> sandbox.safaricom.co.ke, PRODUCTION ->
# api.safaricom.co.ke), so going live is a tenant config flip, not a redeploy.
# MPESA_CALLBACK_BASE_URL=https://api.your-host.co.ke
#
# DEV-ONLY: a shortcode the SIMULATED provider reports for a tenant that has no
# stored credential yet, so a freshly provisioned dev tenant still resolves
# something plausible. The real Daraja provider NEVER falls back to it — a
# tenant without its own active credentials is refused, because settling one
# shop's sale into a shared till would break Option A. Setting this in
# production has no effect on any real payment.
# MPESA_SHORTCODE=174379

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KRA eTIMS (Task 4.1).
#
# WHAT IS HERE: only which IMPLEMENTATION to construct, plus a dev-only simulator
# switch. Both are properties of this DEPLOYMENT.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE, AND NEVER WILL BE: the tenant's KRA PIN (tin),
# branch id, device serial number, communication key (cmcKey) or auth token. Those
# are properties of a TENANT and live encrypted in that tenant's own credential
# store, entered by the owner through PUT /v1/integrations/ETIMS/credentials.
#
# ANGAZA IS NEVER THE KRA INTEGRATOR. Each tenant self-onboards on eTIMS under
# their own PIN. A process-wide eTIMS credential would mean every tenant filing
# under one PIN — a tax misdeclaration against third parties, not merely a
# misconfiguration — so there is no env fallback and no shared signer. A tenant
# without active credentials simply cannot sign, which is harmless: eTIMS is
# OPT-IN PER SALE and a sale is validly finalized without it.
#
#   ETIMS_PROVIDER: "simulated" (default, dev) or "oscu" (real KRA).
# DEV-ONLY here: ETIMS_SIM_OUTCOME.
ETIMS_PROVIDER=simulated
# DEV-ONLY: force every SIMULATED submission to one outcome, for driving a whole
# scenario without editing request data. "sign" (default) | "reject" | "offline".
# Ignored entirely under ETIMS_PROVIDER=oscu. Per-request markers still win: a
# customer PIN starting REJECT/OFFLINE steers one invoice (see simulated-provider.ts).
ETIMS_SIM_OUTCOME=sign
